1
00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:03.870
In this
lesson, you'll build a SQL agent.

2
00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:07.107
You'll then observe situations
where the model hallucinates.

3
00:00:07.574 --> 00:00:09.409
Let's go take a look.

4
00:00:09.409 --> 00:00:13.346
So, let's first take a look at the SQL
workflow with Llama 3.

5
00:00:13.413 --> 00:00:15.715
The user asks a question.

6
00:00:15.715 --> 00:00:19.152
The model takes in that question
and produces SQL,

7
00:00:19.152 --> 00:00:22.856
that SQL is run against a database
and then the user sees the response.

8
00:00:23.256 --> 00:00:27.193
So, an example is asking
who's the highest paid NBA player.

9
00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:30.964
And the answer from Llama 3
is to answer this question

10
00:00:30.964 --> 00:00:34.834
and we can use the following SQLite
query and produces a SQL query here.

11
00:00:35.802 --> 00:00:38.838
And you always want to start
with something easiest to iterate on

12
00:00:38.838 --> 00:00:40.173
to improve the performance of the model.

13
00:00:40.173 --> 00:00:42.742
So let's start with prompt engineering.

14
00:00:42.742 --> 00:00:46.112
Iteration is key for this step,
and probably every single step

15
00:00:46.112 --> 00:00:47.847
you'll be working with today.

16
00:00:47.847 --> 00:00:50.750
So for SQL, a one-pro tip is to include

17
00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:53.753
the schema and include its formatting.

18
00:00:53.953 --> 00:00:57.023
What I mean here is
let's take a look at this prompt.

19
00:00:57.057 --> 00:00:58.925
So who is the highest NBA player?

20
00:00:58.925 --> 00:01:00.260
The user's question.

21
00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:03.963
This is some prompt says you're an NBA
analyst with 15 years of experience

22
00:01:03.963 --> 00:01:05.432
running complex SQL queries.

23
00:01:05.432 --> 00:01:08.401
So is this telling the model
what kind of setting

24
00:01:08.401 --> 00:01:11.704
they're in, who they are,
and then adding an appropriate schema.

25
00:01:11.704 --> 00:01:13.540
So you see the schema here.

26
00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:16.776
It's showing,
you know the different columns, the types,

27
00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:18.378
and then it provides an example.

28
00:01:18.378 --> 00:01:19.879
And why is this important?

29
00:01:19.879 --> 00:01:21.081
Well let's just take a look at

30
00:01:21.081 --> 00:01:24.084
you know here it says team name, jersey, age
height, weight.

31
00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:25.652
Let's take a look at height here.

32
00:01:25.652 --> 00:01:29.823
If the height text actually
didn't have the space for six foot

33
00:01:29.923 --> 00:01:33.493
seven inches, then that's
a completely different format, right.

34
00:01:33.493 --> 00:01:34.861
So you would want the model

35
00:01:34.861 --> 00:01:38.865
to be producing pretty different SQL
so that it actually executes correctly.

36
00:01:38.865 --> 00:01:40.433
So that's an example of why

37
00:01:40.433 --> 00:01:44.337
adding the formatting here or an example
is actually pretty critical.

38
00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:45.772
And you can see it just iterating.

39
00:01:45.772 --> 00:01:48.775
We're removing those examples
and the model will do worse.

40
00:01:49.075 --> 00:01:50.243
So start with prompt engineering.

41
00:01:50.243 --> 00:01:53.313
Iterate here on on what
you want to put in this system prompt.

42
00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:58.852
So out of the box, the LLM as you can
tell, produces a lot more than just SQL.

43
00:01:58.852 --> 00:01:59.552
It produces

44
00:01:59.552 --> 00:02:01.855
you know, to answer this question
we can use the following SQL

45
00:02:01.855 --> 00:02:05.792
like query produces the query
and then explains the query a little bit.

46
00:02:05.792 --> 00:02:08.795
It produces way
more than that SQL statement.

47
00:02:08.795 --> 00:02:12.999
And that makes it hard for you to then
go take that SQL statement and execute it

48
00:02:12.999 --> 00:02:13.800
against your database.

49
00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:16.903
You need to go, you know, maybe
do another model call, maybe do something

50
00:02:16.903 --> 00:02:21.241
to extract and parse out that SQL query
that you ultimately want.

51
00:02:21.474 --> 00:02:26.474
So instead, you can apply structured
output to ensure the model only outputs

52
00:02:26.579 --> 00:02:31.579
SQL and instead outputs it, for example
in a dictionary format or Json format.

53
00:02:32.552 --> 00:02:33.920
So you have a SQLite query

54
00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:37.357
here, is the key
and only the SQL query coming out.

55
00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:40.960
That way you can programmatically
then use that SQL query to execute

56
00:02:41.127 --> 00:02:42.061
against your database.

57
00:02:42.061 --> 00:02:45.064
And in the lab
you'll explore exactly how to enforce

58
00:02:45.064 --> 00:02:48.301
a structured output
with 100% format accuracy.

59
00:02:49.302 --> 00:02:52.372
Next, is diagnosing
these nasty little hallucinations.

60
00:02:52.372 --> 00:02:56.576
So again, the SQL query
can either be invalid or malformed.

61
00:02:56.609 --> 00:02:59.913
For example,
the salary format can be wrong.

62
00:02:59.946 --> 00:03:04.717
So here again the salary is casted
as a string which is malformed.

63
00:03:04.717 --> 00:03:07.921
It is a valid SQL query,
but when it runs it gets the wrong output.

64
00:03:07.921 --> 00:03:12.921
It executes to Saddiq Bey, who makes about
4.5 million in this database.

65
00:03:13.159 --> 00:03:15.962
At least,
I think he is earning a bit more now,

66
00:03:15.962 --> 00:03:17.964
but instead
it should actually be Steph Curry.

67
00:03:17.964 --> 00:03:21.434
So when you cast it here
instead as an integer, the salary,

68
00:03:21.734 --> 00:03:25.238
you know, with a dollar sign, removing
that as an integer, you're able to

69
00:03:25.238 --> 00:03:26.573
then sort an order

70
00:03:26.573 --> 00:03:30.243
by that number much more correctly
than if you were ordering a string.

71
00:03:31.177 --> 00:03:33.246
So diagnosing hallucinations

72
00:03:33.246 --> 00:03:36.749
is a very iterative process,
just like everything you'll see here.

73
00:03:37.116 --> 00:03:40.620
As you improve
the model, you'll continue to just dig

74
00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:42.488
for what those hallucinations are

75
00:03:42.488 --> 00:03:46.426
and what the boundary of correct
and incorrect is for the model.

76
00:03:46.793 --> 00:03:49.495
And this is a very important, skill

77
00:03:49.495 --> 00:03:52.999
because it will actually help you point
the LLM in the right direction.

78
00:03:52.999 --> 00:03:56.636
Give the LLM a north star to climb
to, to tell it,

79
00:03:56.636 --> 00:03:59.872
you know, hey, you need to improve here
and it will improve for you.

80
00:03:59.872 --> 00:04:01.441
It's very good at optimizing.

81
00:04:01.441 --> 00:04:04.911
It just needs your help
on knowing what to optimize for.

82
00:04:05.278 --> 00:04:07.447
So get good at this stuff.
This is an important one.

83
00:04:07.447 --> 00:04:11.117
It'll help you actually be able
to create the best models,

84
00:04:11.117 --> 00:04:14.153
because you have now tools
to remove those hallucinations.

85
00:04:15.088 --> 00:04:17.323
Great. So now let's move on to the lab.

